lia_dev: Quick one — hitting a weird error when importing the JS SDK in an app-router page (Edge runtime). Root message: 'Module is server-only' when building for edge.

sam_engineer: Which SDK version and how are you importing it? ESM/CJS mismatch often causes this.

lia_dev: sdk@1.3.2. In page.tsx I have:
```tsx
// app/page.tsx
import { generate } from 'redwood-inference'; // example
export default async function Page() {
  const out = await generate('hi');
  return <div>{out}</div>;
}
```
Build fails with: "Error: You attempted to import a module that can only run on the server" during edge build.

maria_devex: Two quick things: 1) app router marks files as server by default, but Edge runtime forbids Node native modules (crypto/fs). 2) Next can pick CJS entrypoints which may require node APIs — that triggers server-only errors when bundling for edge.

sam_engineer: Yup. We ship both CJS and ESM. Next sometimes prefers the cjs 'main' field. For edge, you must ensure the package exposes a pure-ESM/browser/edge entry (no native requires). Do you see any "require('crypto')" in the SDK bundle?

dan_engineer: Confirmed — the SDK runtime has a conditional require for node/crypto fallback. Works in node server but breaks in edge. We need an edge-safe build that uses Web Crypto (subtle) instead.

lia_dev: Any short-term workaround I can use in the app to unblock? Dynamic import? move to server action?

maria_devex: Workarounds: 1) Keep SDK use inside a server action or route that runs on node (not edge). 2) If you must call from client, use a browser/edge-specific build path: import('redwood-inference/edge') or import('redwood-inference/browser') if we publish one. 3) For app-router client components, do runtime dynamic import inside useEffect so it only runs client-side (but not for SSR/edge). Example:
```tsx
'use client';
import React, {useEffect, useState} from 'react';
export default function ClientWidget(){
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  useEffect(()=>{
    (async ()=>{
      const sdk = await import('redwood-inference/browser');
      const r = await sdk.generate('hello');
      setText(r);
    })();
  },[]);
  return <div>{text}</div>
}
```

sam_engineer: Also try forcing module resolution to the ESM build by adding a top-level "exports" or "module" field in package.json (we should do this in the SDK). Next will prefer that for modern builds.

lia_dev: TS typings are noisy too — when I import dynamically TS still complains that types aren't available at compile time.

maria_devex: For types-only imports use `import type {Client} from 'redwood-inference';` and then dynamic import for runtime. That separates TS from runtime.

dan_engineer: Action item for SDK: create an explicit `browser`/`edge` entrypoint, remove node native requires from that build, and add docs for Next.js app-router nuances (server vs client + edge). Will open PR.

lia_dev: Perfect. Could we also add a small sample app showing app-router + edge-safe client usage? I can contribute one.

sam_engineer: Please do. Also logging: deploy-bot reports a Canary build failure earlier — unrelated but FYI. :robot_face:

deploy-bot: Canary build 5123 failed for branch feature/edge-sdk (link: https://ci.example/5123)

maria_devex: Outstanding — I'll add a short DX note in the SDK README about: prefer ESM/module export, use edge-specific entrypoint, and prefer server actions for secret calls.

lia_dev: Thanks all. I'll move the call into a server action as a stopgap and try the dynamic import demo. Keep me posted on the PR. :thumbsup:
